  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 145 PM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nA wetter pattern looks to take shape beginning Sunday and continuing \ninto next week. The aforementioned high pressure shifts over to \nsouth-central to southeast CONUS, while a trough pushes onshore \nacross western CONUS. The placement of these two features puts a \nbulk of AZ and NM in a convergence zone where subtropical moisture \nis easily advected into our region. Sunday looks to be the first of \nseveral days of increased precipitation chances. Storms look to \ninitially favor the higher terrain from the central mountain chain \nwestward, before moving into the lower elevations through the \nevening hours. A speed max rounding the trough to our northwest may \nprovide right entrance region synoptic forcing through Sunday \nevening into Monday morning, which may allow for rain to continue \nthroughout the overnight hours. This overnight rainfall, should it \noccur, would likely leave widespread cloud cover and stable air into \nMonday, which can significantly affect precipitation chances, \nrainfall intensity, and temperatures. Much of the same can be said \nfor each day next week, which makes the forecast confidence in the \nfiner details degrade each day given the conditional properties. In \ngeneral, the overall pattern of moisture convergence is likely to \nremain throughout a good portion of next week, leading to consistent \ndays of higher precipitation chances. LREF PWAT values reach or \nexceed the 95th percentile each day, showing the favored environment \nfor rainfall. A consistent question mark (outside of the conditional \nproperties above) remains the possibility of a surface boundary or \ncold front pushing into eastern NM sometime during the middle of the \nweek. A boundary such as this would likely increase precipitation \nchances across eastern NM. While several fine details remain fuzzy, \nthe upcoming pattern appears favorable for much needed rainfall.",
